{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfujx4wsZVXB9UJDXFvAt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"My verdict is **queue-with-edge**: none of the 29 rows contradicts the spine, none asks for work that is already done, and nothing needs consolidating. I added three hard `needs` edges. The verdict is in `d603855cb` with the `WI: WI-903` trailer, and the pre-commit hooks passed.\n\n**Edges:**\n- **WI-798 needs WI-887 (MAJOR).** Both rows can be claimed today, and both change what identifies a retained session in `session_keep.py`. Today a record is keyed on family and route, and the home is per family. WI-798 moves to one home per account, and WI-887 re-keys records on route plus scope. Neither row puts the account in the key. If both were built at once, two accounts using one route would share a single record but launch under two different homes. A session started under one account would then be resumed under the other's. WI-887 already sorts first, and this order keeps WI-800's path shorter than the reverse.\n- **WI-810 needs WI-902 (MINOR).** WI-810 retires `intake.py sweep` (LLR-265). WI-902 amends that sweep so a close can name a successor row that is already queued. Without the edge, WI-902 could end up amending retired rows. This mirrors WI-875's edge from WI-810 to WI-856.\n- **WI-811 needs WI-878 (MINOR).** WI-811's in-loop dispute coverage depends on citing a dispute verdict. In the loop, each rework round numbers its findings from F1 again. That reuse is exactly what WI-878 fixes, so WI-811 should be built on top of it.\n\n**Notes for you, with no edge added:**\n- **WI-901 cites the wrong requirement.** It names LLR-246 under SR-208, but `disposition_values` belongs to LLR-167 (under SR-144, SR-146 and SR-148). WI-851 rewrites LLR-246 and SR-208, and can be claimed the same day. I suggest re-anchoring WI-901 when it is claimed.\n- **WI-833 and WI-851 still name WI-828's walk.** Both say the walk covers \"a squash or merge landing\". Under decision D-015, adding each rule to the shared rider list from WI-892 covers that clause, and WI-893 brings the hand-merge path to every rule later. I didn't make either row wait on WI-893: that would hold WI-851, and with it WI-800 and WI-807 to WI-810, behind the owner's OI-113 ruling.\n\nWI-850 and WI-851 overlap only in text (each adds a rule to the shared list), which a rebase resolves, as WI-875 found. I left the `.requested` file uncommitted, because the adjudication entry point commits it."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":508,"cache_read_input_tokens":168118,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":508},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b4225769-fda3-4bea-bcc1-075cd9af4d08","uuid":"42c6ab6e-1a29-4922-99b6-f1756a675a93","timestamp":"2026-10-11T03:29:41.497Z","request_id":"req_011Cfujx4eWgghd6GjFT3bPF"}
